      "id": "PERF",
      "title": "Performance determinants and biomechanics",
      "statement": "Risk that coaches and athletes rely on guesswork because the mechanics and physiology of strongman events are only partly described.",
      "evidence": "developing",
      "summary": "Biomechanics is the best-studied area of the sport. A 2019 systematic review found 11 studies and called out missing data for the yoke walk, atlas stone, tire flip, vehicle pull, and log lift {hindle2019}. Since then, the stone lift and yoke walk have been described in both men and women {hindle2021stone} {hindle2021yoke}, log diameter has been shown to change force output in the push press {renals2018}, and shoulder muscle activity has been recorded before and after a real competition {studnicki2026}. MRI of one World's Strongest Man and deadlift champion found leg muscle volume roughly double that of untrained men, with tendon size not keeping pace {balshaw2024}. Most studies still use small groups of resistance-trained men rather than competitive strongman athletes, and 2D video rather than 3D motion capture {hindle2019methods}.",
