      "id": "TRAIN",
      "title": "Training and competition preparation",
      "statement": "Risk that training, tapering, and preparation practices are passed on by tradition rather than tested.",
      "evidence": "developing",
      "summary": "Surveys describe how strongman athletes train {winwood2011}, taper {winwood2018taper}, and warm up on competition day {winwood2019comp}. In the taper survey, 87% of 454 athletes used a taper, which lasted 8.6 days on average and cut training volume by about 45%. A 2026 systematic review found similar patterns across the weight-lifting sports {winwood2026}. Psyching-up strategies have been mapped and tested in strength athletes, including strongwomen {cusimano2025} {cusimano2026}, and one study compared conditioning methods in powerlifting and strongman athletes {androulakis2018}. What is missing are controlled training trials in strongman athletes themselves. The strongman training studies that exist mostly recruited rugby players {winwood2015} {winwood2012}.",
